From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Crunch41 <70764418+Crunch41@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 04:01:27 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] Log room activity and transport diagnostics

Upstream's console log is written for emulator debugging: uptime
timestamps, source file, line and function on every line, and ANSI
colours. In a container log, what matters is who joined, left, chatted
and played, and when.

Console and log file lines now look like this:

  [10:23:45] JOIN  | [1.2.3.4] PlayerName has joined. (1/16)
  [10:23:45] PING  | [1.2.3.4] PlayerName RTT 172ms
  [10:23:46] GAME  | PlayerName is playing Mario Kart 8 Deluxe (3.0.3)
  [10:24:10] CHAT  | PlayerName: gg
  [10:27:57] STAT  | [1.2.3.4] PlayerName session join_rtt 172ms last_rtt 180ms peak_rtt 210ms game_pkts 41800 up_holds 12 (2950ms, max 260ms) gaps 14 (max 410ms, 2 unheld) burst_max 46 duration 4m12s
  [10:27:57] LEAVE | [1.2.3.4] PlayerName has left. (0/16)
  [10:28:01] Network <Warning> Dropping malformed LDN packet (3 bytes)

- Local wall-clock time (TZ) instead of uptime; no source location and
  no colour codes. Warnings and errors keep their class and level.
- The console is flushed after every line, so docker logs is current.
- Upstream logs in place on whichever thread logs (room, announce,
  main), with nothing guarding the backends: the file backend's byte
  count and its enabled flag were raced. Writes to the backends are now
  serialised with a mutex.
- Join, leave, kick and ban lines show the player count and room size.
- A member's RTT is logged when it joins (PING), and a STAT line before
  it leaves: RTT at join, last and peak RTT, what the room saw of the
  member's game packets (below) and session length. ENet resets a
  peer's statistics before reporting its disconnect, so RTT is kept
  from the join and from DIAG samples; with DIAG off, last and peak
  equal the join value.

What the room can see of a member's game packets on their way to it:

- Eden clients send everything reliably on one ENet channel, so when a
  datagram from a member is lost, ENet holds everything the member sends
  after it until the client resends it, one retransmit timeout later
  (about 50 ms on a short route, 250 ms across the Pacific). Every
  other player sees that member freeze, then jump. The room watches the
  channel's queue of held commands on every pass of its loop: a hold of
  10 ms or more is one up_hold (shorter ones are fragmented packets
  still arriving). This is the member's uplink loss as it affects play.
- A gap of 100 ms to 5 s between a member's game packets with no hold
  in it ("unheld") means the member sent nothing: their PC or emulation
  stalled (shader compiles, frame drops). Longer gaps are idle time.
- burst_max is the most game packets in a row less than 2 ms apart; a
  hold or a stall ends in a burst.

What it cannot see: loss on its own unreliable packets to members
(nothing acknowledges them), and anything in the game. ENet's packet
loss figure is the share of the room's sends it had to resend; in the
unreliable modes only pings and control messages are ever resent, so
it read about 0% on a path losing 1%. It is no longer reported as loss,
only as "resend" in reliable mode.

EDEN_ROOM_DIAG_INTERVAL_SEC (default 0 = off, up to 3600) adds a DIAG
report every that many seconds: relay mode and settings once at start,
then member RTT, holds and gaps, relayed packets and bytes (in total and
since the last report), drops by reason (oversize, malformed, budget,
unknown IP) and packet sizes; a line per member while game packets
flow; and an advice line that says only what those numbers show: who
is held or stalling, and, in the unreliable modes, what the room cannot
see. It never calls the transport clean.

Tests: tests/test_room_protocol.py checks the JOIN, LEAVE, CHAT, GAME,
PING and STAT lines and the DIAG lines in the room's output, that a
clean path shows no holds, and, for a member behind a link that delays
its packets by 30 ms, loses one and then pauses, that DIAG and STAT show
the loss as a hold and the pause as an unheld gap and name the member.
